<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 38 gate 3. DROP A FILE THAT IS NOT YET ON THIS MACHINE, and get the
  clip when it lands - as one undo step, on the track the drop named, and
  nowhere at all when the fetch fails or the target went away.

  Everything here runs through production code. `media-browser-drag` builds the
  REAL QMimeData the panel's startDrag() builds - now "media:<uri>" under
  application/x-smaragd-resource, because the source reports NeedsFetch - and
  hands it to the REAL SMVActualView::dropEvent, whose ONE new branch (five
  lines) calls smediadrop::placeWhenLocal. The placement is the SAME
  SAddSampleAction the `file:` branch has always submitted; there is no new
  action type anywhere in gate 3.

  THE SOURCE IS `testdelay` (SDelayedLocalSource), registered only under
  SMARAGD_MEDIA_TEST_SOURCE=1, which the CTest entry sets. It reads the same
  committed fixture tree the local provider does, reports NeedsFetch, and
  copies after a configurable delay with a configurable failure - the same
  instinct as twtestclap. Gate 3 has no network, so without it the deferred
  branch could only be tested against a real server's timing.

  THE CACHE IS PER CASE AND EMPTIED FIRST: the CTest entry sets
  SMARAGD_MEDIA_CACHE_DIR into this case's own test-output directory, and
  `media-test-source clearCache="1"` empties it. Both are needed. The knob keeps
  another process under `ctest -j4` out of it; the clear keeps the PREVIOUS RUN
  of this same case out of it, without which every `fetches=` below would read
  one number on a cold box and another on the second run - the exact shape of a
  gate that passes once and then quietly stops meaning anything.

  WHAT IT PINS

  1. AC 3 + AC 6. Two drops of an UNCACHED ref, issued back to back onto two
     DIFFERENT tracks. Neither places anything before the fetch lands
     (delayMs=800 - the drop handler returns first, which is what "deferred"
     MEANS), and when it does there are TWO clips, each at exactly the frame
     and on exactly the track its own drop named. Not one, and not three: the
     pending list is keyed by PLACEMENT, not by ref.

     TWO DIFFERENT TRACKS ON PURPOSE, and this cost a flake to learn: two
     concurrent deferred placements land in FETCH-COMPLETION order, which is
     not the order they were dropped in. With both on one track the case
     asserted `clip 0,0 startTime=48000` and got 192000 in 4 runs out of 10 -
     the assertion was about the arrival ORDER, which nothing promises, rather
     than about the placement, which everything does. One clip per track states
     the real claim and is exact on every run.
  2. AC 2. A THIRD drop of the same ref, now cached, places SYNCHRONOUSLY - the
     clip is asserted BEFORE media-drop-wait runs at all - and `fetches` does
     not move. That is the no-re-fetch claim end to end, through a real
     provider, rather than through a counter on the cache.
  3. Each placement is exactly ONE undo step: three drops, three undos, no
     clips left.
  4. AC 4. A fetch told to FAIL places nothing, logs an error, and leaves the
     clip count where it was.
  5. AC 5a (trap T18). The target track is REMOVED while the fetch is in
     flight. Nothing is placed - not on the removed track and not on whichever
     track now sits at the index-path the drop resolved to. This is the whole
     reason a pending placement holds the target's IDENTITY: an index-path is a
     position in a tree the user is free to edit during a 40 MB download, and a
     clip landing silently on the WRONG lane is worse than not landing at all.
     `remove-track` is undoable and PINS the removed track on the action object,
     so the track is still ALIVE here - which is exactly why the test is
     REACHABILITY FROM THE ROOT and not liveness.
  6. AC 7 (trap T11). With a SAVED project the fetched file is copied to
     <projectdir>/media/ and the saved .qxp stores a RELATIVE path. A clip left
     pointing into the machine-local cache would serialise as an absolute path
     that means nothing on the next machine, which is the whole of what
     SFilePathRef exists to prevent.

  NOT PINNED HERE, and said plainly rather than implied:

   * AC 5 (the project CLOSED or SWITCHED cancels a pending placement) is NOT
     reachable from a script. No verb closes or switches the current project:
     `load-project` loads INTO the existing SProject object rather than
     swapping it, so neither SApplication::setCurrentProject (where the cancel
     is wired) nor the pending entry's own project-identity check can fire.
   * No real network fetch. `testdelay` copies a local file - the deferred
     BRANCH is what is under test, never the physics of a download.
   * "Places nothing" is asserted as observed behaviour. There is no sanitizer
     build in this repo, so nothing here is a claim about memory safety.

  SETTINGS (trap T6). This case writes media/lastSourceId and
  media/lastPath/testdelay, and restores both to their SOpt defaults, so
  smaragd.ini comes back with identical CONTENT. It is RUN_SERIAL.
  media/lastPath/testdelay is EXCLUSIVELY this case's. media/lastSourceId is
  shared with media_browser_browse / media_browser_search /
  media_browser_drag_local - all four are RUN_SERIAL and all four restore it to
  "local", so no run can ever observe another's value. This case deliberately
  touches neither media/categoryMask nor media/searchRecursive: the defaults
  (audio, non-recursive) are exactly what it wants, so there is nothing to
  restore.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="media_drop_deferred">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>   <!-- 0: the deferred + cached placements -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>   <!-- 1: the failed fetch, and the copy      -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>   <!-- 2: removed mid-fetch (AC 5a)           -->

    <set-option key="media/lastPath/testdelay" value="../media/lib"/>
    <media-test-source delayMs="800" failPath="" reset="1" clearCache="1"/>
    <media-browser-source sourceId="testdelay"/>
    <assert-media-browser mode="browse" rowCount="3" contains="kick.wav"/>

    <!-- (1) AC 3 + AC 6: two drops of an UNCACHED ref, back to back, onto two
         different tracks (see the header: their completion ORDER is not
         promised, their PLACEMENT is). -->
    <media-browser-drag name="kick.wav" trackPath="0" timePos="48000"/>
    <media-browser-drag name="kick.wav" trackPath="1" timePos="192000"/>
    <!-- Neither has placed anything yet: the fetch takes 800 ms and the drop
         handler returned immediately. THIS is the deferred branch. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,0" expectReject="true"/>

    <media-drop-wait waitMs="15000" placed="2" failed="0" abandoned="0"
                     fetches="2"/>
    <!-- AC 7, the OTHER half: the project has no file yet, so the clip
         references the machine-local cache and that is said out loud rather
         than discovered on the next machine (T11). -->
    <assert-log level="warn" contains="the project has never been saved"
                minCount="1"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="48000" duration="192000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,0" startTime="192000" duration="192000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,1" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- (2) AC 2: the ref is CACHED now, so this one places SYNCHRONOUSLY. The
         assertion is made BEFORE media-drop-wait, so a deferred placement
         would fail it. -->
    <media-browser-drag name="kick.wav" trackPath="0" timePos="288000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" startTime="288000" duration="192000"/>
    <media-drop-wait waitMs="5000" placed="3" failed="0" abandoned="0"
                     fetches="2"/>

    <!-- (3) THREE drops, THREE undo steps. -->
    <undo count="3"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,0" expectReject="true"/>
    <redo count="3"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="48000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" startTime="288000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,0" startTime="192000"/>

    <!-- (4) AC 4: a fetch that FAILS places nothing and logs an error. -->
    <media-test-source failPath="snare"/>
    <media-browser-drag name="snare.wav" trackPath="1" timePos="336000"/>
    <media-drop-wait waitMs="15000" placed="3" failed="1" abandoned="0"
                     fetches="2"/>
    <assert-log level="error" contains="drop: fetch of" minCount="1"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,1" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- (5) AC 5a / T18: the target track is REMOVED while the fetch runs.
         snare.wav is still uncached (its only fetch failed), so this is a real
         pending placement. -->
    <media-test-source failPath=""/>
    <media-browser-drag name="snare.wav" trackPath="2" timePos="48000"/>
    <remove-track trackPath="2"/>
    <media-drop-wait waitMs="15000" placed="3" failed="1" abandoned="1"
                     fetches="3"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" contains="the target track was removed"
                minCount="1"/>
    <!-- Nothing landed anywhere: track 0 still has exactly its two clips and
         track 1 exactly its one. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,2" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,1" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- (6) AC 7 / T11: with a SAVED project the fetched file is copied into
         <projectdir>/media/ and the .qxp stores a RELATIVE path. -->
    <save-project path="../../build/test-output/media_drop_deferred/media_drop_deferred.qxp"/>
    <media-browser-drag name="snare.wav" trackPath="1" timePos="384000"/>
    <media-drop-wait waitMs="15000" placed="4" failed="1" abandoned="1"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,1" startTime="384000"/>
    <save-project path="../../build/test-output/media_drop_deferred/media_drop_deferred.qxp"/>
    <assert-file-contains path="../../build/test-output/media_drop_deferred/media_drop_deferred.qxp"
                          text="media/snare.wav"/>

    <!-- Restore every key this case writes (T6). -->
    <set-option key="media/lastSourceId" value="local"/>
    <set-option key="media/lastPath/testdelay" value=""/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
